WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:06.000
From an art heist gone horribly wrong to the&nbsp;
most messed-up body horror nightmare in years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.120
these are the best thrillers&nbsp;
you already missed this year.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.880
Gerard Butler's action thriller, "Plane,"&nbsp;
got buried early in 2023 due to little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.900
marketing and no momentum heading into&nbsp;
its January release. That's a shame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.480
because it's honestly one of the actor's best.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.800
This action-packed adventure follows&nbsp;
pilot Brodie Torrance as he's forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.000
to crash-land a plane onto a remote island,&nbsp;
where his passengers are taken hostage by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.900
local rebel faction. With the help of&nbsp;
one of his riders — a former soldier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:37.854
accused of murder — Brodie must rescue his&nbsp;
passengers and help them get off the island.

00:00:37.935 --> 00:00:42.082
"I'm telling you that the&nbsp;
way home is on this plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.140
I got you this far, I just need&nbsp;
you to trust me this one more bit."

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.580
"Plane" features one of most harrowing&nbsp;
crash-landing sequences you'll see in a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.720
and it perfectly sets the tone for the&nbsp;
incredibly tense fights that follow. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.520
the great suspense and strong chemistry between&nbsp;
Butler and actor Mike Colter help lead the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.980
to an undeniably epic firefight finale. Sure,&nbsp;
Butler's action thriller flicks have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.080
little one-note in the past — but despite the&nbsp;
simple title, "Plane" is far from generic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.825
providing viewers with the kind of thrill&nbsp;
ride that audiences are always searching for.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.500
Anyone that's seen director Brandon&nbsp;
Cronenberg's previous films "Antiviral"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.400
and "Possessor" know that he's a modern&nbsp;
master of sickeningly twisted thrills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.580
much like his father David. The&nbsp;
younger Cronenberg's latest project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:31.200
"Infinity Pool," takes viewers on a luxury&nbsp;
vacation that takes a sinister turn.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.660
Struggling writer James — played by Alexander&nbsp;
Skarsgård — gets the death penalty when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:41.760
accidentally kills a man with his car. He's given&nbsp;
a bizarre proposition, though — for a fee, James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.280
will be cloned, and the clone will be executed in&nbsp;
his place. It's a choice that continues to haunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.720
him as he descends into a depraved criminal&nbsp;
underworld that pushes him to his limits.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.640
With "Infinity Pool," Cronenberg once again&nbsp;
manages to elicit plenty of "icks" from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.240
audience. There are moments that'll leave&nbsp;
viewers tense and disturbed — especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.480
when Mia Goth's performance and&nbsp;
that signature Cronenberg body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.440
horror are involved. "Infinity Pool"&nbsp;
may have slipped past wider audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.000
but that doesn't mean that its incredible crime&nbsp;
thrills and gripping horrors should be ignored.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.051
"Maybe think of it as a gift."

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.680
Back in 2018, Sony Screen Gems delivered a&nbsp;
breakout hit with "Searching," in which John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.720
Cho gives an unforgettably gripping performance&nbsp;
through some stellar webcam thrills and a twisty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.820
mystery. It's possible many fans of the first&nbsp;
film don't know that an equally great sequel hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:35.460
theaters in 2023 — largely because it's called&nbsp;
"Missing" rather than "Searching 2," and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.860
buried by a January release. Like "Searching,"&nbsp;
"Missing" utilizes its web-based premise in smart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.140
ways as young June uncovers disturbing truths&nbsp;
about her family after her mother disappears.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.820
"Missing" is an unbelievable thrill ride&nbsp;
that's guaranteed to leave viewers shocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:56.580
by its unexpected turns and wild twists. Storm&nbsp;
Reid's lead performance perfectly leads audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.540
through all the chilling discoveries June&nbsp;
makes, keeping viewers hooked right up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.580
until the supremely tense and explosive finale.&nbsp;
Given how popular "Searching" was when it hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.480
it's baffling that "Missing" was just&nbsp;
thrown under the rug by Sony. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.900
doesn't mean that this is a movie worth&nbsp;
forgetting about, though — far from it.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:18.300
Whether he's playing a lighthouse keeper,&nbsp;
the Green Goblin, or Jesus Christ himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.500
Willem Dafoe never fails to turn in&nbsp;
a standout performance — and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.540
definitely applies for 2023's "Inside."&nbsp;
Still, fans of the actor may have missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.160
the heist-turned-survival flick that stars&nbsp;
Dafoe alongside… pretty much no one else.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.800
Dafoe flies solo as Nemo, an art thief whose&nbsp;
latest job goes terribly wrong after a security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.660
system malfunction leaves him trapped inside&nbsp;
a luxury apartment. While a penthouse suite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.740
might not seem like such a terrible place to&nbsp;
get stuck, it quickly becomes a living hell:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.600
his escape options are severely limited, and&nbsp;
the security system constantly fluctuates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:57.240
apartment's temperature between sweltering heat&nbsp;
and frigid cold. As Nemo's situation continues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.965
to grow dire, his sanity starts to slip — and&nbsp;
that's when Dafoe's performance truly captivates.

00:04:01.965 --> 00:04:11.145
"I have no energy I have I'm dying&nbsp;
I'm dying I'm dying I'm dying I'm dy—"

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.660
"Inside" provides some good survival&nbsp;
thrills and inventive moments that'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.620
get viewers hooked from the start, but&nbsp;
it's Dafoe's one-man show that'll really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:24.300
leave an impact. Nemo's slow descent into&nbsp;
madness and disjointed reality makes for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:28.740
harrowing watch — one that will keep you&nbsp;
guessing up until the very end. "Inside"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:34.020
got completely overshadowed by "Shazam: Fury&nbsp;
of the Gods" when it hit theaters, but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.700
doesn't mean that audiences should miss out on&nbsp;
its uniquely intriguing premise and performance.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.920
Not every thriller needs to provide painful&nbsp;
moments or chilling turns to be a highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:49.140
engaging ride, and Guy Ritchie's criminally&nbsp;
underseen "Operation Fortune: Ruse de Guerre"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:54.900
is proof. This star-studded adventure follows&nbsp;
a team of spies as they recruit a movie star in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.340
an effort to stop an evil billionaire arms&nbsp;
dealer. "Operation Fortune" is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.840
Ritchie's take on a James Bond spy adventure&nbsp;
— and it makes for a hilariously fun time.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.940
Ritchie's signature style of comedy works&nbsp;
perfectly in creating some very funny and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.800
engaging moments from the highly talented cast&nbsp;
— which includes Jason Statham, Aubrey Plaza,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:19.500
Josh Hartnett, Hugh Grant, and Cary Elwes.&nbsp;
The action is fast and fierce, and there's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.180
shortage of wild gunfights and thrilling chases.&nbsp;
Based on the film's poor box-office performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.620
"Operation Fortune" clearly wasn't on a&nbsp;
lot of radars when it hit theaters — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.580
better late than never when it comes to&nbsp;
watching this highly entertaining romp.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.560
Since the pandemic delayed the release of&nbsp;
"Operation Fortune," two Guy Ritchie films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.820
ended up coming out in the first half of&nbsp;
2023. The second one was "The Covenant,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.020
which made it to theaters in the U.S. just&nbsp;
a month and a half after the director's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.700
previous effort — and while it's also a quality&nbsp;
watch, it couldn't be more different in tone.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.260
"The Covenant" is set during the War in&nbsp;
Afghanistan and stars Jake Gyllenhaal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:00.660
and Dar Salim. Sergeant John Kinley and his&nbsp;
Afghan interpreter Ahmed help innocent people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.920
survive against the Taliban's growing shadow&nbsp;
— but when Ahmed's family becomes threatened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:07.260
Kinley must take matters into his own hands.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:11.820
Along with Gyllenhaal and Salim delivering&nbsp;
great performances, Ritchie helms some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.600
incredibly suspenseful firefights that are&nbsp;
bound to leave viewers hanging on the edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.880
of their seats throughout all the close-calls&nbsp;
and unsettling suspense. The entire last act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.320
is a true fight for survival, which makes for&nbsp;
a very satisfying and cathartic conclusion.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:30.300
"The Covenant" arguably got overshadowed by&nbsp;
the breakout success of "Evil Dead Rise,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.180
but that simply means that&nbsp;
Ritchie has a killer double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.500
feature full of amazing lesser-known&nbsp;
thrills waiting for people to experience.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:43.140
When it first debuted, critics praised Daniel&nbsp;
Goldhaber's activist thriller "How to Blow Up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:48.480
a Pipeline" for its solid script and impressive&nbsp;
ensemble cast. Despite its instantly compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.280
title, though, the reverse heist flick didn't&nbsp;
gain much popularity in mainstream circles.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:59.580
Inspired by author Andreas Malm's 2021 book of&nbsp;
the same name, "How to Blow Up a Pipeline" follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.560
eight young people as they plot to destroy&nbsp;
oil infrastructure in West Texas. The film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.420
environmentalist message and philosophical&nbsp;
conundrums are front and center — after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:14.040
our heroes never quite decide whether what&nbsp;
they're doing counts as terrorism or not.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.820
"American empire calls us terrorists,&nbsp;
then we're doing something right."&nbsp;

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:23.220
From a narrative perspective, the movie's most&nbsp;
significant achievement is its ability to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:28.440
viewers invested in the individual stories of&nbsp;
all eight members over its 100-minute runtime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:33.120
Things only become more nerve-shredding as the&nbsp;
group goes deeper into crafting their plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.220
the stakes ratchet up, and things start to go&nbsp;
horribly wrong. "How to Blow Up a Pipeline" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:43.140
full of shockingly explosive moments and&nbsp;
gripping turns that'll leave viewers stunned.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:49.320
It's no surprise that an international thriller on&nbsp;
Netflix can end up being overlooked — with so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:54.540
content on the service, it's easy for something to&nbsp;
get lost in the shuffle. Of course, that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:59.520
mean that subscribers should miss out on "Kill&nbsp;
Boksoon," an incredibly fun South Korean thriller.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.560
Gil Bok-soon is both an assassin and&nbsp;
a single mom, whose life and flawless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.420
success rate on the job are put into jeopardy&nbsp;
when she finds herself in a kill-or-be-killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:14.220
conflict. Director Sung-hyun Byun shows&nbsp;
some great prowess in crafting thrilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:19.320
and engaging action sequences that aren't afraid&nbsp;
to spill some blood, keeping viewers hooked on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:24.540
every punch and slash. Whether it's through brutal&nbsp;
hand-to-hand combat or with a variety of weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:29.460
the titular assassin absolutely makes her mark&nbsp;
on her foes — and the audience, in the process.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.780
"Kill Boksoon" features a great cast of&nbsp;
characters with distinct personalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:39.120
and a slow-growing conflict that boils over into&nbsp;
a shocking finale. It's a shame that great movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.560
like "Kill Boksoon" can easily be missed — so&nbsp;
you should definitely add it to your watchlist.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.680
Since it was also dropped onto Netflix without&nbsp;
much fanfare, fans of the BBC One show "Luther"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.000
might not realize that a film continuation&nbsp;
called "The Fallen Sun" hit the streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.100
service in early March. The film sees Idris&nbsp;
Elba reprise his role as disgraced detective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:04.140
John Luther as he tries to right the wrongs&nbsp;
of his past and capture the serial killer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.880
continues to haunt him. Elba returning to this&nbsp;
role will definitely feel satisfying for fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.240
but the movie is also an excellent&nbsp;
gateway for newcomers to learn about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.380
one of the actor's most iconic roles.&nbsp;
"The Fallen Sun" is a very solid serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:20.160
killer hunt with twists and turns that'll&nbsp;
keep you guessing at how things will end.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:26.100
If Idris Elba wasn't enough of a draw, "The Fallen&nbsp;
Sun" also features great supporting performances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:31.860
from Andy Serkis and Cynthia Erivo. There's rarely&nbsp;
a moment to catch your breath, and you never truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:36.420
know when things are about to get brutal. Whether&nbsp;
you're a longtime "Luther" fan or someone looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:41.040
to get a taste for what it's all about, "The&nbsp;
Fallen Sun" is a thriller worth checking out.

